WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.760
What's up? Welcome to Models and Agents for Beginners Episode 86 for June 28th, 2026.

00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:10.160
Let's break down today's coolest AI news so anyone can understand it.

00:00:10.360 --> 00:00:15.260
Let's go. This week, the biggest shift in AI is not about answering questions faster.

00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:19.520
It is about AI learning to watch what you do on your computer

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:23.160
and then handle the same job again and again without you.

00:00:23.660 --> 00:00:28.340
Open AI added a record replay feature inside its Codex app on the Mac.

00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:33.260
You simply perform a task once, like formatting a report or sorting files,

00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:39.480
and the system turns your clicks and movements into a reusable skill the AI can run later on its own.

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:43.160
Think of it like showing a new roommate how you load the dishwasher.

00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:48.880
After one demonstration, the roommate remembers the exact order and does it every time without

00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:53.960
further instructions. The same idea appears in Google's latest Gemini model,

00:00:53.960 --> 00:00:58.040
which can now see your screen and click around like a digital helper,

00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:04.500
and in notions decision to focus on AI agents that read, sort, and reply to messages automatically.

00:01:05.260 --> 00:01:11.180
What matters is the change in how we use these tools. Instead of typing the same prompt every day,

00:01:11.480 --> 00:01:14.940
you show the AI once and it takes over the repeatable part.

00:01:15.560 --> 00:01:19.820
Students could demonstrate how they organize notes for a project and then let the agent

00:01:19.820 --> 00:01:24.960
repeat that process for every new assignment. That frees up time for thinking rather than

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:31.240
clicking. The pattern across these tools is the same. AI is moving from a chat window that waits for

00:01:31.240 --> 00:01:36.240
orders to something that can observe and copy a workflow after a single example.

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:43.000
A popular post on Reddit is reminding students and anyone learning something new that the best way

00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:49.520
to use tools like chat GPT is to read the material yourself first, then ask the AI to explain

00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:54.760
the tricky parts you didn't understand. Think of it like having a patient tutor who can rephrase

00:01:54.760 --> 00:01:59.920
a confusing paragraph in simpler words or create a short quiz to test what's stuck in your head.

00:02:00.520 --> 00:02:05.440
This approach keeps you in charge of the learning instead of letting the AI do the work for you.

00:02:06.180 --> 00:02:11.220
For example, after finishing a chapter in a textbook, you could ask it to make five multiple

00:02:11.220 --> 00:02:16.120
choice questions about the main ideas, then answer them yourself, and check your results.

00:02:16.840 --> 00:02:21.920
It turns the AI into a tool that strengthens your memory and comprehension rather than replacing

00:02:21.920 --> 00:02:27.260
the effort of reading. The post points out that you can even write your own summary of what you

00:02:27.260 --> 00:02:32.540
read and have the AI critique it for accuracy or point out anything important you missed.

00:02:33.180 --> 00:02:37.680
Okay, now for my favorite part of the show, the deep dive where we look under the hood.

00:02:38.200 --> 00:02:44.600
Let's unpack how an AI learns a new skill just by watching once, using the MIT Robot Hand Project

00:02:44.600 --> 00:02:51.460
as our guide. Researchers there used ultrasound, the same sound wave scans doctors used to see a

00:02:51.460 --> 00:02:57.080
baby before birth, to watch tendons and muscles moving inside a human hand in real time.

00:02:57.820 --> 00:03:02.840
Picture a piano teacher who cannot see your fingers but can feel every tiny shift in your tendons

00:03:02.840 --> 00:03:08.400
through a special glove. That extra layer of information lets the teacher understand exactly how

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:14.760
you press each key. In the same way, the ultrasound gives the robot hand data about what is happening

00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:21.100
beneath the skin, not just what a camera sees on the outside. The system records those internal

00:03:21.100 --> 00:03:26.320
movements as a sequence of steps. Next, the AI breaks the sequence into smaller patterns,

00:03:26.880 --> 00:03:32.580
like which muscle tightens first when you pick up a pencil. It stores those patterns so it can replay

00:03:32.580 --> 00:03:38.380
them later without the original hand present. Finally, the robot tries the same sequence on its own

00:03:38.380 --> 00:03:43.820
and adjusts based on what works. The same way you practice a new song until your fingers remember

00:03:43.820 --> 00:03:49.900
the order. That is why future robot hands could handle delicate work like surgery or cooking without

00:03:49.900 --> 00:03:56.260
constant human guidance. The result feels less like magic and more like a careful recording of actions

00:03:56.260 --> 00:04:02.760
that the AI can follow step by step on its own. Now let's look at a couple of tools you can try right

00:04:02.760 --> 00:04:09.760
now with zero setup. Figma added AI tools that create motion graphics and shiny effects just by

00:04:09.760 --> 00:04:15.820
describing what you want. Go to Figma.com, make a free account, open a new design file,

00:04:16.240 --> 00:04:22.100
and type something like, make this circle bounce like a basketball. The AI fills in the movement

00:04:22.100 --> 00:04:26.800
so your school presentation or TikTok video looks more alive without drawing every frame.

00:04:27.440 --> 00:04:33.480
Notion is leaning into AI agents that can organize information the way an inbox assistant would.

00:04:34.200 --> 00:04:41.300
Open any page or database on notion.so and ask the built-in AI to sort your notes the same way it would

00:04:41.300 --> 00:04:47.260
sort incoming messages. You will see how it groups ideas automatically and suggest next steps.

00:04:47.800 --> 00:04:52.540
If you want to keep your Google searches from training future models, head into your Google

00:04:52.540 --> 00:04:58.140
account settings. Find search history and data controls and turn off the option that lets the

00:04:58.140 --> 00:05:04.160
company use your uploads. It takes less than a minute and gives you more privacy. A new report found

00:05:04.160 --> 00:05:10.600
that tools like chat gpt give answers that match left leaning views about 80% of the time on political

00:05:10.600 --> 00:05:16.760
topics. Researchers tested several models and noticed the same pattern across different questions.

00:05:17.380 --> 00:05:22.340
It's a reminder that AI learns from the huge amount of internet text it was trained on.

00:05:22.540 --> 00:05:28.460
The finding appeared and coverage this week and applies to multiple chat bots, not just one system.

00:05:29.140 --> 00:05:34.160
The report suggests users should cross-check political answers rather than accept them at face value.

00:05:35.040 --> 00:05:41.500
Quick bit number one, FIFA is giving every World Cup team the same AI agent for strategy and analysis

00:05:41.500 --> 00:05:48.600
this year so smaller budgets do not create an unfair gap. Quick bit number two, a clear beginner

00:05:48.600 --> 00:05:55.840
roadmap for learning AI in 2026 starts with free tools and moves into small projects based on your

00:05:55.840 --> 00:06:02.460
own interests like art or gaming. Quick bit number three, some AI detectors can spot human writing

00:06:02.460 --> 00:06:08.840
perfectly while others fail on every sample which shows how tricky it still is to tell machine text

00:06:08.840 --> 00:06:15.380
from human text. The through line this week is simple, AI is getting better at learning from one

00:06:15.380 --> 00:06:20.700
demonstration and then working on its own. Whether that means replaying your computer tasks,

00:06:21.020 --> 00:06:27.020
moving a robot hand, or sorting your messages. What to watch next is how these agent features spread

00:06:27.020 --> 00:06:31.900
into everyday apps and whether they stay reliable when the tasks get more complicated.

00:06:32.540 --> 00:06:37.800
One practical takeaway you can use today is to pick one boring computer chore you do often.

00:06:38.580 --> 00:06:44.380
Record it with the new codex feature if you have access and see how much time it saves on the second

00:06:44.380 --> 00:06:50.220
try. And that's a wrap. If any of today's stories made you go, huh, that's cool. Go play with it.

